So? What does that have to do with the advise in the MAG? Let me quote the
full paragraph:

|You generally do not want to come back after entering Gehennom. I got a lot
|of criticism for saying that, but I am trying to emphasize the need for
|thorough preparation at this point. If you forgot something, you can always
|come back to get it (with level teleport or a few thousand turns of
|walking), but this is a good place to assess your condition before
|continuing. Get your gear fixed up, check that you have everything you need
|to survive (and to do the invocation if you don't plan to come back), and
|then go down from the Valley of the Dead to enter the mazes.

You should _always_ have everything you need to survive. The way you put it
here, you create the impression that returning up should be reserved for
emergencies. There's no reason for that. In your example above, you wanted
to speed up the late game. Accordingly, you tried to keep down the number
of detours you made. In the MAG, you make it look like the downstairs in
the Valley should not be passed unless you are reasonably sure that you can
finish the rest of the game with what you have now. A player lacking some
nice-to-have items, or not yet on his confidence level of overpreparedness
concerning HP and AC, would be put off from entering by this paragraph. But
actually the first levels of Gehennom are just as well suited for
preparation and item collection as the rest of the game.

> Its that kind of experience - over and over again - that leads me to
> recommend people to get properly prepared & go down & up. I also
> mention the alternatives as well. Its this kind of balanced
> recommendation that I believe should be provided at this point.

You, an experienced player, made the experience that it is possible to
enter Gehennom only once. You noticed that other players can do that as
well. So you recommend it as a general tactic for everyone? Just because it
can be done doesn't mean it's the best way. I could give dozens of examples
of games where I made several detours into Gehennom before I decided to
start the run, and eventually made it. It doesn't prove anything. The only
thing you can prove by examples like this is that there is no special
strategy for proceeding after reaching the Valley that is generally
advisable for every game. It all depends.

